::ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>2</sub>(x)=5. {
= x = 32#Correct. Rewrite as x = 2<sup>5</sup>.
~ x = 10#This multiplies the base and output instead of using exponents.
~ x = 7#This adds the base and output.
~ x = 3#This subtracts the base from the output.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>3</sub>(x - 1)=2. {
~ x = 9#This forgets to add 1 after solving x - 1 = 9.
= x = 10#Correct. Rewrite as x - 1 = 3<sup>2</sup>, so x = 10.
~ x = 7#This adds base and output incorrectly.
~ x = 2#This uses the logarithm output as the solution.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2<sup>x</sup> = 16. {
~ x = 8#This divides 16 by 2.
~ x = 14#This subtracts 2 from 16.
= x = 4#Correct. Since 16 = 2<sup>4</sup>, the exponents are equal.
~ x = 32#This multiplies 16 by 2.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 5<sup>x</sup> = 11 using logarithms. {
~ x = log(5)/log(11)#This reverses the ratio.
~ x = 11/5#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x = 55#This multiplies the base and output.
= x = log(11)/log(5)#Correct. Change of base gives x as the logarithm ratio.
}

::ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L06_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.1<br><br>Question:<br>Why must x = -2 be rejected for log<sub>4</sub>(x)? {
= The logarithm input is not positive.#Correct. Logarithmic inputs must be greater than zero.
~ The base is not positive.#The base 4 is positive and valid.
~ The exponent is too large.#The issue is the input value, not exponent size.
~ Negative numbers are always solutions.#Negative logarithm inputs are not allowed here.
}

